  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">List Cultures is still an early-impact scholarly book, but the record already shows a small yet meaningful footprint: one citation in OpenAlex, one teaching-related listing, and alignment with the SDG area of Quality Education. The book is also available through open-access channels, which may support wider reading and reuse.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The clearest signal in this record is an emerging scholarly uptake: the book has been cited once, indicating that it has begun to enter related research conversations. That citation appears in a 2022 article in Revista Galega de Filolox\u00eda, suggesting use in humanities-oriented work that connects literature, pedagogy, and linguistic or cultural mediation.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The book\u2019s thematic profile points to broad interdisciplinary relevance across media studies, sociology, philosophy, aesthetics, history, and computer-related concepts. That mix fits a work concerned with the cultural and material life of lists, and it helps explain why the book may resonate across humanities and social-science contexts rather than within a single narrow field.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">There is also a teaching signal in the record: one OCW listing references the book\u2019s subject matter in the context of an Introduction to Psychology resource. While this is not evidence of formal course adoption, it does suggest conceptual reach into educational settings where lists, culture, and cognition intersect.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">From an access perspective, the book is openly available in at least some form, and that openness can lower barriers for readers in research and teaching environments. The record also links the work to Quality Education, reinforcing a plausible educational and policy-relevant dimension, though the evidence remains modest and should be read as an early signal rather than established large-scale impact.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, List Cultures shows the first signs of scholarly and pedagogical attention, with its impact profile still thin but coherent: one citation, one teaching reference, and open availability. The present record suggests a book beginning to circulate in academic and classroom-adjacent settings, with further citation and teaching evidence likely needed before stronger conclusions can be drawn.</p>",
